# Did you ever hear about the vigette brothers?


Two Vigette brothers, masterminds of shade,

Each guarded well the cipher that he made.

The elder aligned his grids with practiced grace,

The younger shifted letters through time and space.

Each swore his method was the true design,

A sacred lock along a grid-drawn line.

When pride collided, brotherhood turned cold,

A bitter war of scholar-kings of old.

Yet from the ashes of their fierce debate,

A strange, new cipher was conceived in hate—

A fused creation born of rivalry,

To forge a lock no simple key could free.

Unlike the standard matrix of the past,

No looping keyword breaks this spell at last.

Instead, the text itself becomes the key—

A running stream of words to set it free.

Prose unlocks prose along a self-drawn thread,

Where decoding follows everywhere the story led.

Now long forgotten, buried out of sight,

The Vigette cipher sleeps in digital night.

No printed books retain their final strain,

No parchment scrolls or legacy remain.

If you would read the secret they betrayed,

And trace the wondrous matrix that they made,

Look to the artifacts where shadows play—

Locked safely in the folder labeled a.
